# Ollama max-load benchmark: qwen3.5:4b, CPU vs dGPU

**Date:** 2026-11-15 · **Benchmark machine:** local-ai · **Ollama:** 0.40.16 · **Model:** qwen3.5:4b · **Runs:** 5 each (cold start)

This is a heavier follow-up to the short refrigerator-prompt test in `bench-ollama-results.md`. It uses a long prompt and a long output, so prompt processing and sustained generation both get properly loaded. Every run generated exactly the same number of tokens, so total times compare directly.

## Result

| Metric (median of 5 runs) | CPU (i5-12600K, 6 threads) | dGPU (GTX 1080 Ti) | dGPU advantage |
|---------------------------|----------------------------|--------------------|----------------|
| Generation speed          | 8.03 tok/s                 | 61.17 tok/s        | **7.6x faster** |
| Prompt processing speed   | 101 tok/s                  | 1,424 tok/s        | **14x faster** |
| Prompt processing time    | 32.38 s                    | 2.29 s             | −30.1 s |
| Generation time           | 127.45 s                   | 16.74 s            | −110.7 s |
| Load time                 | 2.05 s                     | 2.28 s             | about the same |
| **Total time per run**    | **162.13 s**               | **21.33 s**        | **7.6x faster** |
| Input / output tokens     | 3,266 / 1,024              | 3,266 / 1,024      | identical work |
| Hit the token limit       | 5 / 5                      | 5 / 5              | as intended |

**The GTX 1080 Ti finishes the same job in about 21 seconds; the CPU takes about 2 minutes 42 seconds.**

The dGPU test was run a second time with temperature and clock logging. Its medians came out within 1% of the first run (61.76 tok/s, 21.39 s total). The log shows why the card slows down from run to run: it heats up to its temperature limit. See [dGPU temperature and clocks](#dgpu-temperature-and-clocks).

The iGPU was not tested this time. See [iGPU](#igpu-not-tested) below.

